 new(107,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U07:L05","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-7/lesson-5/preparation.html","f3d266624557f861516f3aaf02ea2e21e3dde03365ce423bb82eabdce3dfcef7",new string[]{"CCSS:2.NBT.A.4","CCSS:2.NBT.B.7"},
 new string[]{"Learning goal: You will practice adding numbers within 1,000.\nThis lesson is optional because it is an opportunity for extra practice that not all classes may need. In Activity 1, you learn stage 7 of the Five in a Row: Addition and Subtraction center. In this new stage, you add within 1,000 without composing.","Key ideas:\n- Add within 1,000\n- This lesson is optional because it is an opportunity for extra practice that not all classes may need\n- Subtraction can represent taking away, comparing quantities, or finding a missing addend; the representation must match the situation.","Example 1: How many do you see? How do you see them?\nApproach: “How did the groups help you to know the value of the blocks?” (We described seeing the same kinds of blocks together. People saw hundreds with hundreds, tens with tens, and ones with ones.)","Step 1: Identify what the task asks you to find or show.\nStep 2: Represent the important quantities or relationships.\nStep 3: Apply the key idea from the lesson.\nStep 4: Explain how the representation supports the result.\nStep 5: Check that the result matches the question.","Common mistake: jumping to an answer before checking that the representation matches the quantities and relationship in the task.","You will practice adding numbers within 1,000. This lesson is optional because it is an opportunity for extra practice that not all classes may need."},
 new string[]{"IM2021 Grade2 Unit7 Lesson5, Center Day 1 (optional). ORIGINAL PUBLISHER TASKS: OPTIONAL original number grouping images: first base-ten diagram 2 hundreds 5 tens 4 ones = 254; public warmup figures include 474 and 676. Center Five in a Row Stage7 adds within 1000 WITHOUT composing; students move a paper clip on each turn, add chosen board numbers, cover the sum and get five in a row. Second activity lets students choose Get Your Numbers in Order, Mystery Number or The Greatest of Them All. Learning objective: Use place-value grouping and a no-carry addition, and differentiate teacher-observed original optional physical game (2 paper clips, 10 two-color counters). MEDIA AND OPEN-WORK LIMITS: Original pictured diagram, game board values, paperclip moves, five-in-a-row win, actual student partner strategy and optional center choices are not reconstructed or auto-graded.","G2 Unit7 source-specific math: Use place-value grouping and a no-carry addition, and differentiate teacher-observed original optional physical game (2 paper clips, 10 two-color counters). Official CCSS references: CCSS:2.NBT.A.4, CCSS:2.NBT.B.7. Independent first-party questions are mathematically aligned but do not substitute for student-created representations or original publisher print materials.","ORIGINAL public student-facing mathematics and material: OPTIONAL original number grouping images: first base-ten diagram 2 hundreds 5 tens 4 ones = 254; public warmup figures include 474 and 676. Center Five in a Row Stage7 adds within 1000 WITHOUT composing; students move a paper clip on each turn, add chosen board numbers, cover the sum and get five in a row. Second activity lets students choose Get Your Numbers in Order, Mystery Number or The Greatest of Them All. First-party new example with a different numerical variant follows the same place-value structure. Teacher separately reviews original physical source work: Original pictured diagram, game board values, paperclip moves, five-in-a-row win, actual student partner strategy and optional center choices are not reconstructed or auto-graded.","1. Read the exact original source lesson mathematics and required materials: OPTIONAL original number grouping images: first base-ten diagram 2 hundreds 5 tens 4 ones = 254; public warmup figures include 474 and 676. Center Five in a Row Stage7 adds within 1000 WITHOUT composing; students move a paper clip on each turn, add chosen board numbers, cover the sum and get five in a row. Second activity lets students choose Get Your Numbers in Order, Mystery Number or The Greatest of Them All. 2. Show the starting hundreds/tens/ones in blocks, expanded form, or a correctly labelled number line. 3. Before adding, predict whether ones compose a ten and/or tens compose a hundred; for the optional center constrain every independent numeric problem to no regrouping. 4. Find the exact sum, justifying each exchange of ten ones for one ten or ten tens for one hundred. 5. Verify the first-party numeric sum using inverse subtraction and record partner methods as educator-reviewed nonnumeric work. 6. Do not claim source-specific outcomes from invented card or picture values.","A frequent error is treating ten ones as a hundred, forgetting to carry from ones into tens, or claiming students won the original physical game without observing its board. Distinguish an addition that composes a ten from one that composes a hundred or both. Original pictured diagram, game board values, paperclip moves, five-in-a-row win, actual student partner strategy and optional center choices are not reconstructed or auto-graded.","IM2021 Grade2 Unit7 Lesson5. Use place-value grouping and a no-carry addition, and differentiate teacher-observed original optional physical game (2 paper clips, 10 two-color counters). Authentic source: OPTIONAL original number grouping images: first base-ten diagram 2 hundreds 5 tens 4 ones = 254; public warmup figures include 474 and 676. Center Five in a Row Stage7 adds within 1000 WITHOUT composing; students move a paper clip on each turn, add chosen board numbers, cover the sum and get five in a row. Second activity lets students choose Get Your Numbers in Order, Mystery Number or The Greatest of Them All. Original pictured diagram, game board values, paperclip moves, five-in-a-row win, actual student partner strategy and optional center choices are not reconstructed or auto-graded."}),
