 new string[]{"Learning goal: You will add a two-digit number to a three-digit number that requires composing a ten when adding by place.\nIn previous grades, you developed fluency with sums of 10 and looked for ways to make a ten as a strategy for adding within 20. You used your understanding of these sums to make sense of composing a ten when adding by place and as a strategy for adding within 100. In this lesson, you use what you know about making a ten to find the sum of a three-digit number and a two-digit number.","Key ideas:\n- Add numbers within 1,000 using place value strategies that include composing a ten\n- In previous grades, you developed fluency with sums of 10 and looked for ways to make a ten as a strategy for adding within 20\n- The addends are the quantities being combined and the sum is the resulting total; the expression must match the representation.","Example 1: Find the value of each expression mentally. \\(28 + 2\\) \\(28 + 12\\) \\(67 + 3\\) \\(67 + 23\\)\nApproach: “Why are \\(28 + 2\\) and \\(67 + 3\\) helpful in finding the value of the other expressions?” (We made ten and then added 1 more ten.)\nExample 2: Find the value of each sum. Set 1 Set 2 Set 3 \\(245 + 15\\) \\(134 + 26\\) \\(351 + 19\\) \\(247 + 23\\) \\(133 + 37\\) \\(356 + 24\\) \\(249 + 31\\) \\(138 + 42\\) \\(355 + 35\\) What patterns did you notice?\nApproach: “What patterns did you notice in each set?” (Each time we could make a ten with the ones. The number of hundreds didn't change. The sums went up by 10.) “How does knowing \\(6 + 4 = 10\\) help you think about \\(536 + 34\\) ?” (I can add \\(530 + 30 = 560\\) . Then add 10 because I know \\(6 + 4 = 10\\) .)","Step 1: Identify the quantities being combined.\nStep 2: Represent each quantity with objects, a drawing, number line, or expression.\nStep 3: Write an addition relationship that matches the representation.\nStep 4: Find the total using an efficient strategy.\nStep 5: Check that the total matches the original quantities.","Common mistake: counting an item twice or combining the wrong quantities. Check the addends and verify the total.","You will add a two-digit number to a three-digit number that requires composing a ten when adding by place. In previous grades, you developed fluency with sums of 10 and looked for ways to make a ten as a strategy for adding within 20."},
 new string[]{"IM2021 Grade2 Unit7 Lesson6, Use a Ten to Add Within 1,000. ORIGINAL PUBLISHER TASKS: Original warm-up 28+2=30,28+12=40,67+3=70,67+23=90; first activity sum table 245+15=260,247+23=270,249+31=280;134+26=160,133+37=170,138+42=180;351+19=370,356+24=380,355+35=390. Original Perfect10 Card Sort: match three-digit and two-digit cards so ones add to exactly ten. Learning objective: Use combinations that make ten to add within 1000; add by place with composing ONE ten and compare partner matches with student justification. MEDIA AND OPEN-WORK LIMITS: Publisher printed Perfect 10 card graphics, students' free card pairings, blocks and explanations require human review. Source protected Cool-down unavailable.","G2 Unit7 source-specific math: Use combinations that make ten to add within 1000; add by place with composing ONE ten and compare partner matches with student justification. Official CCSS references: CCSS:2.NBT.B.5, CCSS:2.NBT.B.7. Independent first-party questions are mathematically aligned but do not substitute for student-created representations or original publisher print materials.","ORIGINAL public student-facing mathematics and material: Original warm-up 28+2=30,28+12=40,67+3=70,67+23=90; first activity sum table 245+15=260,247+23=270,249+31=280;134+26=160,133+37=170,138+42=180;351+19=370,356+24=380,355+35=390. Original Perfect10 Card Sort: match three-digit and two-digit cards so ones add to exactly ten. First-party new example with a different numerical variant follows the same place-value structure. Teacher separately reviews original physical source work: Publisher printed Perfect 10 card graphics, students' free card pairings, blocks and explanations require human review. Source protected Cool-down unavailable.","1. Read the exact original source lesson mathematics and required materials: Original warm-up 28+2=30,28+12=40,67+3=70,67+23=90; first activity sum table 245+15=260,247+23=270,249+31=280;134+26=160,133+37=170,138+42=180;351+19=370,356+24=380,355+35=390. Original Perfect10 Card Sort: match three-digit and two-digit cards so ones add to exactly ten. 2. Show the starting hundreds/tens/ones in blocks, expanded form, or a correctly labelled number line. 3. Before adding, predict whether ones compose a ten and/or tens compose a hundred; for the optional center constrain every independent numeric problem to no regrouping. 4. Find the exact sum, justifying each exchange of ten ones for one ten or ten tens for one hundred. 5. Verify the first-party numeric sum using inverse subtraction and record partner methods as educator-reviewed nonnumeric work. 6. Do not claim source-specific outcomes from invented card or picture values.","A frequent error is treating ten ones as a hundred, forgetting to carry from ones into tens, or claiming students won the original physical game without observing its board. Distinguish an addition that composes a ten from one that composes a hundred or both. Publisher printed Perfect 10 card graphics, students' free card pairings, blocks and explanations require human review. Source protected Cool-down unavailable.","IM2021 Grade2 Unit7 Lesson6. Use combinations that make ten to add within 1000; add by place with composing ONE ten and compare partner matches with student justification. Authentic source: Original warm-up 28+2=30,28+12=40,67+3=70,67+23=90; first activity sum table 245+15=260,247+23=270,249+31=280;134+26=160,133+37=170,138+42=180;351+19=370,356+24=380,355+35=390. Original Perfect10 Card Sort: match three-digit and two-digit cards so ones add to exactly ten. Publisher printed Perfect 10 card graphics, students' free card pairings, blocks and explanations require human review. Source protected Cool-down unavailable."}),
