 new string[]{"Learning goal: You will add two-digit numbers and three-digit numbers and compose a ten or a hundred.\nIn previous lessons, you used your understanding of place value to add and subtract within 100 by composing and decomposing tens. You learned that a hundred is a unit that is composed of 10 tens and composed and decomposed hundreds to name the values of base-ten blocks. In this lesson, the number choices and the use of base-ten blocks encourage you to look for ways to add by place and to anticipate where composing a unit might be needed before adding (MP7). - Representation Activity 2: Walk About and Add","Key ideas:\n- Add numbers within 1,000 using place value strategies that include composing a ten or hundred\n- In previous lessons, you used your understanding of place value to add and subtract within 100 by composing and decomposing tens\n- Measurement units must be equal in size and placed or interpreted without gaps, overlaps, or a shifted starting point.","Example 1: How many do you see? How do you see them?\nApproach: “How are these images the same? How are you different?” (You each represent the same number. You have different amounts of hundreds, tens, and ones.) “How could you tell when you could compose a ten or a hundred?” (When there were ten of a unit. I saw two rows of 5 tens, so I knew I had enough to count it as 1 hundred.)\nExample 2: Find the value of each expression. Show your thinking. Use base-ten blocks if it helps. \\(364 + 28\\) \\(364 + 82\\) Compare your thinking with your partner.\nApproach: “How was finding the value of the expressions the same? How was it different?” (In both of them you have to make a unit. In the first one you made a ten with 10 ones and in the second one you made a hundred with 10 tens.) “How did ___ represent composing a ten to find the value of \\(364 + 28\\) ?” “How did ___ represent composing a hundred to find the value of \\(364 + 82\\) ?”","Step 1: Identify what needs to be measured.\nStep 2: Choose the required unit and correct starting point.\nStep 3: Place or interpret equal-size units accurately.\nStep 4: Count or calculate the units and record the measure.\nStep 5: Check the endpoint, scale, and unit label.","Common mistake: using unequal units, leaving gaps, overlapping units, or starting at the wrong point. Check the unit and endpoints.","You will add two-digit numbers and three-digit numbers and compose a ten or a hundred. In previous lessons, you used your understanding of place value to add and subtract within 100 by composing and decomposing tens."},
 new string[]{"IM2021 Grade2 Unit7 Lesson7, Compose a Larger Unit PLC Activity. ORIGINAL PUBLISHER TASKS: Source base-ten diagrams 3 hundreds 5 tens 3 ones,3 hundreds 4 tens 13 ones and 2 hundreds 15 tens 3 ones ALL represent 353. Activity1 364+28=392 composes a ten; 364+82=446 composes a hundred. Activity2 Walk About and Add requires actual A three-digit/B two-digit cards and partner predictions of composing ten or hundred; synthesis 428+42=470. Learning objective: Compose a ten OR a hundred; relate ten ones to one ten and ten tens to one hundred, distinguish carry units, and justify before finding total. MEDIA AND OPEN-WORK LIMITS: Actual source diagrams, physical place value blocks, card partner pairing and student explanations are teacher-only; no invented official card values.","G2 Unit7 source-specific math: Compose a ten OR a hundred; relate ten ones to one ten and ten tens to one hundred, distinguish carry units, and justify before finding total. Official CCSS references: CCSS:2.NBT.B.5, CCSS:2.NBT.B.7, CCSS:2.NBT.B.8. Independent first-party questions are mathematically aligned but do not substitute for student-created representations or original publisher print materials.","ORIGINAL public student-facing mathematics and material: Source base-ten diagrams 3 hundreds 5 tens 3 ones,3 hundreds 4 tens 13 ones and 2 hundreds 15 tens 3 ones ALL represent 353. Activity1 364+28=392 composes a ten; 364+82=446 composes a hundred. Activity2 Walk About and Add requires actual A three-digit/B two-digit cards and partner predictions of composing ten or hundred; synthesis 428+42=470. First-party new example with a different numerical variant follows the same place-value structure. Teacher separately reviews original physical source work: Actual source diagrams, physical place value blocks, card partner pairing and student explanations are teacher-only; no invented official card values.","1. Read the exact original source lesson mathematics and required materials: Source base-ten diagrams 3 hundreds 5 tens 3 ones,3 hundreds 4 tens 13 ones and 2 hundreds 15 tens 3 ones ALL represent 353. Activity1 364+28=392 composes a ten; 364+82=446 composes a hundred. Activity2 Walk About and Add requires actual A three-digit/B two-digit cards and partner predictions of composing ten or hundred; synthesis 428+42=470. 2. Show the starting hundreds/tens/ones in blocks, expanded form, or a correctly labelled number line. 3. Before adding, predict whether ones compose a ten and/or tens compose a hundred; for the optional center constrain every independent numeric problem to no regrouping. 4. Find the exact sum, justifying each exchange of ten ones for one ten or ten tens for one hundred. 5. Verify the first-party numeric sum using inverse subtraction and record partner methods as educator-reviewed nonnumeric work. 6. Do not claim source-specific outcomes from invented card or picture values.","A frequent error is treating ten ones as a hundred, forgetting to carry from ones into tens, or claiming students won the original physical game without observing its board. Distinguish an addition that composes a ten from one that composes a hundred or both. Actual source diagrams, physical place value blocks, card partner pairing and student explanations are teacher-only; no invented official card values.","IM2021 Grade2 Unit7 Lesson7. Compose a ten OR a hundred; relate ten ones to one ten and ten tens to one hundred, distinguish carry units, and justify before finding total. Authentic source: Source base-ten diagrams 3 hundreds 5 tens 3 ones,3 hundreds 4 tens 13 ones and 2 hundreds 15 tens 3 ones ALL represent 353. Activity1 364+28=392 composes a ten; 364+82=446 composes a hundred. Activity2 Walk About and Add requires actual A three-digit/B two-digit cards and partner predictions of composing ten or hundred; synthesis 428+42=470. Actual source diagrams, physical place value blocks, card partner pairing and student explanations are teacher-only; no invented official card values."}),
