 new string[]{"Learning goal: You will add within 1,000 using place value-based strategies by composing both a ten and a hundred.\nIn previous lessons, you related composing a ten and a hundred when adding within 1,000. You found sums that required composing one larger unit when adding by place.","Key ideas:\n- Add a two-digit number to a three-digit number using place value strategies that include composing units\n- In previous lessons, you related composing a ten and a hundred when adding within 1,000\n- Measurement units must be equal in size and placed or interpreted without gaps, overlaps, or a shifted starting point.","Example 1: How many do you see? How do you see them?\nApproach: “How were the images the same? How were you different?” (The first two had the same number of tens. I saw a group of three ones in each image. You each showed different values. The last image had more than 10 tens.) “What would you need to do to show the value of the third image with the least amount of blocks?” (If you were using blocks, you could exchange 10 tens for 1 hundred. Use one hundred instead of 10 tens.)\nExample 2: Find the value of each sum. Show your thinking. Use base-ten blocks if it helps. \\(273 + 18\\) \\(273 + 81\\) \\(273 + 88\\) What was the same and different about the sums?\nApproach: “What was the same and different about what you did to find the value of each sum?” (We had to make a ten and a hundred for the last one. For the first 2, we just had to make a ten or a hundred.)","Step 1: Identify the quantities being combined.\nStep 2: Represent each quantity with objects, a drawing, number line, or expression.\nStep 3: Write an addition relationship that matches the representation.\nStep 4: Find the total using an efficient strategy.\nStep 5: Check that the total matches the original quantities.","Common mistake: counting an item twice or combining the wrong quantities. Check the addends and verify the total.","You will add within 1,000 using place value-based strategies by composing both a ten and a hundred. In previous lessons, you related composing a ten and a hundred when adding within 1,000."},
 new string[]{"IM2021 Grade2 Unit7 Lesson8, Compose Tens and Hundreds to Add. ORIGINAL PUBLISHER TASKS: Original Activity1 three related sums 273+18=291 (compose one ten),273+81=354 (compose one hundred),273+88=361 (compose both a ten and a hundred); students compare how carrying differs, represent valid base-ten strategies and interpret peers' methods. Learning objective: Add a two-digit number to a three-digit number and compose BOTH a ten AND a hundred; no rigid prescribed student strategy, compare multiple correct methods. MEDIA AND OPEN-WORK LIMITS: Original publisher images, drawings, actual student work, pairwise explanations and unavailable protected Cool-down are educator-reviewed.","G2 Unit7 source-specific math: Add a two-digit number to a three-digit number and compose BOTH a ten AND a hundred; no rigid prescribed student strategy, compare multiple correct methods. Official CCSS references: CCSS:2.NBT.B.7, CCSS:2.NBT.B.9. Independent first-party questions are mathematically aligned but do not substitute for student-created representations or original publisher print materials.","ORIGINAL public student-facing mathematics and material: Original Activity1 three related sums 273+18=291 (compose one ten),273+81=354 (compose one hundred),273+88=361 (compose both a ten and a hundred); students compare how carrying differs, represent valid base-ten strategies and interpret peers' methods. First-party new example with a different numerical variant follows the same place-value structure. Teacher separately reviews original physical source work: Original publisher images, drawings, actual student work, pairwise explanations and unavailable protected Cool-down are educator-reviewed.","1. Read the exact original source lesson mathematics and required materials: Original Activity1 three related sums 273+18=291 (compose one ten),273+81=354 (compose one hundred),273+88=361 (compose both a ten and a hundred); students compare how carrying differs, represent valid base-ten strategies and interpret peers' methods. 2. Show the starting hundreds/tens/ones in blocks, expanded form, or a correctly labelled number line. 3. Before adding, predict whether ones compose a ten and/or tens compose a hundred; for the optional center constrain every independent numeric problem to no regrouping. 4. Find the exact sum, justifying each exchange of ten ones for one ten or ten tens for one hundred. 5. Verify the first-party numeric sum using inverse subtraction and record partner methods as educator-reviewed nonnumeric work. 6. Do not claim source-specific outcomes from invented card or picture values.","A frequent error is treating ten ones as a hundred, forgetting to carry from ones into tens, or claiming students won the original physical game without observing its board. Distinguish an addition that composes a ten from one that composes a hundred or both. Original publisher images, drawings, actual student work, pairwise explanations and unavailable protected Cool-down are educator-reviewed.","IM2021 Grade2 Unit7 Lesson8. Add a two-digit number to a three-digit number and compose BOTH a ten AND a hundred; no rigid prescribed student strategy, compare multiple correct methods. Authentic source: Original Activity1 three related sums 273+18=291 (compose one ten),273+81=354 (compose one hundred),273+88=361 (compose both a ten and a hundred); students compare how carrying differs, represent valid base-ten strategies and interpret peers' methods. Original publisher images, drawings, actual student work, pairwise explanations and unavailable protected Cool-down are educator-reviewed."})
